data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core 9 (.NET 9), the Cosmos DB provider dropped synchronous I/O by default, but offered a **temporary** `EnableSyncOverAsync()` opt-in. In EF Core 11 (.NET 11), **that opt-in is removed entirely**. All four of your sync calls will throw `InvalidOperationException` at runtime:

| Sync method (breaks) | Async replacement |
|---|---|
| `ToList()` | `ToListAsync()` |
| `First(...)` | `FirstAsync(...)` |
| `Count()` | `CountAsync()` |
| `SaveChanges()` | `SaveChangesAsync()` |

## The fix

Convert the repository to async:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- `Add()` stays synchronous — it only tracks the entity in memory; no I/O occurs until `SaveChangesAsync()`.
- Callers must propagate `async/await` up the call chain (controllers, services, etc.).
- Remove the `EnableSyncOverAsync()` line from your `DbContext` configuration — it no longer compiles.
